1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,259
This is the Sun.

2
00:00:01,259 --> 00:00:03,443
Shrunk to a single grain of sand.

3
00:00:03,443 --> 00:00:06,753
Keep that scale, and something impossible happens.

4
00:00:06,753 --> 00:00:13,042
The entire observable universe, every galaxy we can see, would fit inside our own solar system.

5
00:00:13,742 --> 00:00:16,439
Today we take that grain of sand, and we walk.

6
00:00:16,439 --> 00:00:20,192
From a tabletop, to the nearest star, to the edge of everything.

7
00:00:20,192 --> 00:00:23,065
And we'll keep a stopwatch running the whole way.

8
00:00:23,765 --> 00:00:24,371
The rule.

9
00:00:24,371 --> 00:00:28,883
The real Sun is about one point three nine million kilometers wide.

10
00:00:28,883 --> 00:00:30,769
Our grain is one millimeter.

11
00:00:30,769 --> 00:00:36,426
So every millimeter on our walk stands for almost one point four million kilometers.

12
00:00:36,426 --> 00:00:38,177
We never change that rule.

13
00:00:38,177 --> 00:00:38,783
Not once.

14
00:00:39,484 --> 00:00:40,908
Here's our trail sign.

15
00:00:40,908 --> 00:00:41,621
Five stops.

16
00:00:41,621 --> 00:00:48,809
The Earth, the edge of the solar system, the nearest star, our galaxy, and the edge of the observable universe.

17
00:00:48,809 --> 00:00:49,522
First stop.

18
00:00:49,522 --> 00:00:50,364
Our own desk.

19
00:00:51,064 --> 00:00:52,574
Put the grain on a table.

20
00:00:52,574 --> 00:00:53,480
Where is Earth?

21
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:54,447
Not touching it.

22
00:00:54,447 --> 00:00:56,925
Earth sits about eleven centimeters away.

23
00:00:56,925 --> 00:00:58,737
About the length of your hand.

24
00:00:58,737 --> 00:01:01,638
And Earth itself is a speck of nine micrometers.

25
00:01:01,638 --> 00:01:03,813
Ten times thinner than a human hair.

26
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:06,351
You could not see it without a microscope.

27
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:11,608
And one whole year, Earth's entire orbit, is a circle about the size of a dinner plate.

28
00:01:12,308 --> 00:01:13,974
Now zoom in on that speck.

29
00:01:13,974 --> 00:01:17,821
The Moon is a smaller speck, a quarter of a millimeter away.

30
00:01:17,821 --> 00:01:23,079
Every human who ever traveled in space stayed inside that quarter of a millimeter.

31
00:01:23,079 --> 00:01:24,233
Every one of them.

32
00:01:24,933 --> 00:01:31,945
Jupiter, the biggest planet, is a tenth of a millimeter wide, and it sits fifty six centimeters from the grain.

33
00:01:31,945 --> 00:01:32,956
Across the desk.

34
00:01:32,956 --> 00:01:34,977
Saturn is just over a meter out.

35
00:01:34,977 --> 00:01:38,578
Neptune, the last planet, is more than three meters away.

36
00:01:38,578 --> 00:01:39,589
Across the room.

37
00:01:40,289 --> 00:01:43,804
And Voyager one, the farthest spacecraft we ever launched?

38
00:01:43,804 --> 00:01:48,411
After forty nine years of flying, it has reached one light day from the Sun.

39
00:01:48,411 --> 00:01:51,259
On our table, that's eighteen point six meters.

40
00:01:51,259 --> 00:01:53,259
It hasn't even left the building.

41
00:01:53,959 --> 00:01:54,887
Stop one, done.

42
00:01:54,887 --> 00:01:59,954
Our odometer reads eighteen meters, and the walk has taken about thirteen seconds.

43
00:02:00,654 --> 00:02:04,367
Before we go on, here's the strangest thing about this scale.

44
00:02:04,367 --> 00:02:04,733
Light.

45
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:08,994
In real life, light from the Sun reaches Earth in about eight minutes.

46
00:02:08,994 --> 00:02:14,350
On our table, it still takes eight minutes, but it only has to cross eleven centimeters.

47
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:16,935
So at this scale, light crawls.

48
00:02:16,935 --> 00:02:18,942
Point two millimeters per second.

49
00:02:18,942 --> 00:02:21,070
Seventy eight centimeters per hour.

50
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:22,833
A garden snail would beat it.

51
00:02:22,833 --> 00:02:27,637
Remember that, because every distance from here on, light needs years to cover.

52
00:02:28,337 --> 00:02:28,928
Stop two.

53
00:02:28,928 --> 00:02:31,160
The real edge of the Sun's family.

54
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:36,216
Far beyond Neptune there's a giant shell of icy bodies called the Oort cloud.

55
00:02:36,216 --> 00:02:41,009
Nobody has ever seen it directly, but long period comets come from there.

56
00:02:41,709 --> 00:02:51,282
NASA puts its inner edge somewhere between two thousand and five thousand times Earth's distance from the Sun, and its outer edge as far as a hundred thousand.

57
00:02:51,282 --> 00:02:58,025
On our walk, the shell starts about two hundred meters from the grain, and reaches almost eleven kilometers out.

58
00:02:58,025 --> 00:03:01,698
The size of a whole town, with a grain of sand in the middle.

59
00:03:02,398 --> 00:03:05,252
Our odometer reads ten point eight kilometers.

60
00:03:05,252 --> 00:03:06,927
About two hours of walking.

61
00:03:06,927 --> 00:03:09,843
And we haven't reached a single other star yet.

62
00:03:10,543 --> 00:03:11,275
Stop three.

63
00:03:11,275 --> 00:03:14,334
Proxima Centauri, the closest star to the Sun.

64
00:03:14,334 --> 00:03:16,794
Four point two four light years away.

65
00:03:16,794 --> 00:03:20,850
At our scale, every light year is six point eight kilometers.

66
00:03:20,850 --> 00:03:25,903
So Proxima is another grain of sand, about twenty nine kilometers from ours.

67
00:03:26,603 --> 00:03:30,891
That's almost six hours of walking, and the whole way there is empty.

68
00:03:30,891 --> 00:03:35,861
Two grains of sand, twenty nine kilometers apart, and almost nothing in between.

69
00:03:35,861 --> 00:03:38,533
That's what our part of the galaxy is like.

70
00:03:38,533 --> 00:03:41,081
Grains of sand, tens of kilometers apart.

71
00:03:41,081 --> 00:03:47,915
Its neighbors, Alpha Centauri A and B, a pair of Sun-like stars, sit just beyond, about thirty kilometers out.

72
00:03:48,615 --> 00:03:53,447
And light, our snail, needs four years and three months to crawl that far.

73
00:03:53,447 --> 00:03:54,818
Same as in real life.

74
00:03:54,818 --> 00:03:56,908
The scale changes the distances.

75
00:03:56,908 --> 00:03:59,389
It never changes the time light takes.

76
00:04:00,089 --> 00:04:00,704
Stop four.

77
00:04:00,704 --> 00:04:01,381
Our galaxy.

78
00:04:01,381 --> 00:04:09,380
The Milky Way is about one hundred thousand light years across, with somewhere between one hundred and four hundred billion stars.

79
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:13,379
At our scale, that's six hundred eighty thousand kilometers wide.

80
00:04:14,079 --> 00:04:16,984
Wider than the distance from Earth to the Moon.

81
00:04:16,984 --> 00:04:18,282
Almost twice as wide.

82
00:04:18,282 --> 00:04:25,143
A disc of sand grains, each one tens of kilometers from its neighbor, that would stretch past the Moon's orbit.

83
00:04:25,143 --> 00:04:29,098
Walking across it, nonstop, would take fifteen and a half years.

84
00:04:29,798 --> 00:04:33,367
And at the center sits its black hole, Sagittarius A star.

85
00:04:33,367 --> 00:04:40,136
About four million times the mass of the Sun, with an event horizon about twenty four million kilometers wide.

86
00:04:40,136 --> 00:04:46,720
On our walk, it's a dark bead less than two centimeters across, a hundred eighty kilometers from our grain.

87
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:49,489
A long drive, with a black marble at the end.

88
00:04:50,189 --> 00:04:52,135
Now the next galaxy, Andromeda.

89
00:04:52,135 --> 00:04:54,647
Two and a half million light years away.

90
00:04:54,647 --> 00:04:57,284
Seventeen million kilometers at our scale.

91
00:04:57,284 --> 00:04:59,105
Forty four trips to the Moon.

92
00:04:59,105 --> 00:05:01,240
Almost four hundred years on foot.

93
00:05:01,939 --> 00:05:03,427
And here's the paradox.

94
00:05:03,427 --> 00:05:06,595
Stars are tiny compared to the gaps between them.

95
00:05:06,595 --> 00:05:10,281
Twenty nine kilometers between two one millimeter grains.

96
00:05:10,281 --> 00:05:13,126
But galaxies are big compared to their gaps.

97
00:05:13,126 --> 00:05:16,489
Andromeda is only about twenty five Milky Ways away.

98
00:05:17,189 --> 00:05:21,325
That's why stars almost never crash into each other, but galaxies do.

99
00:05:21,325 --> 00:05:27,979
Hubble measurements in twenty twelve predicted that Andromeda will collide with us in about four billion years.

100
00:05:27,979 --> 00:05:33,614
A newer study in twenty twenty five says it's closer to a coin flip over the next ten billion.

101
00:05:33,614 --> 00:05:37,690
Either way, the stars themselves would mostly slide past each other.

102
00:05:38,390 --> 00:05:39,011
Last stop.

103
00:05:39,011 --> 00:05:41,248
The edge of the observable universe.

104
00:05:41,248 --> 00:05:51,873
Its light set out almost fourteen billion years ago, but space kept stretching, so that edge is now about forty six and a half billion light years away in every direction.

105
00:05:52,573 --> 00:05:56,463
At our scale, that's three hundred sixteen billion kilometers.

106
00:05:56,463 --> 00:06:02,864
Walking nonstop, at five kilometers per hour, you would arrive in about seven point two million years.

107
00:06:02,864 --> 00:06:06,629
About twenty four times longer than our species has existed.

108
00:06:06,629 --> 00:06:11,837
In a car at a hundred kilometers an hour, still three hundred sixty thousand years.

109
00:06:12,537 --> 00:06:14,835
And remember, that edge is not a wall.

110
00:06:14,835 --> 00:06:17,920
It's only as far as light has had time to reach us.

111
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:20,884
There's almost certainly more universe beyond it.

112
00:06:20,884 --> 00:06:24,514
We just can't see it, and we covered why in episode sixteen.

113
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:27,235
Our walk ends at the edge of what we can see.

114
00:06:27,935 --> 00:06:32,782
But three hundred sixteen billion kilometers is a distance we can actually picture.

115
00:06:32,782 --> 00:06:37,571
It's about two thousand one hundred times the real distance from Earth to the Sun.

116
00:06:37,571 --> 00:06:39,498
Which brings us back to the hook.

117
00:06:40,198 --> 00:06:49,582
So take the shrunken universe, about four thousand two hundred times the Earth Sun distance from side to side, and set it down in the real solar system.

118
00:06:49,582 --> 00:06:52,298
Voyager one is at one hundred seventy three.

119
00:06:52,298 --> 00:06:55,323
The Oort cloud reaches out to a hundred thousand.

120
00:06:56,023 --> 00:07:03,223
Every galaxy we can see, shrunk until the Sun is a grain of sand, would fit inside the Sun's own cloud of comets.

121
00:07:03,223 --> 00:07:05,389
The universe, inside our backyard.

122
00:07:06,089 --> 00:07:06,850
Now flip it.

123
00:07:06,850 --> 00:07:11,483
Shrink the whole observable universe until it's the size of planet Earth.

124
00:07:11,483 --> 00:07:13,324
How big is the Milky Way now?

125
00:07:13,324 --> 00:07:14,720
About fourteen meters.

126
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:18,718
The size of a bus, parked somewhere on a planet sized universe.

127
00:07:19,418 --> 00:07:20,234
And the Sun?

128
00:07:20,234 --> 00:07:22,275
Two hundredths of a nanometer.

129
00:07:22,275 --> 00:07:24,724
Smaller than a single hydrogen atom.

130
00:07:24,724 --> 00:07:30,234
Our whole star, invisible inside one atom's width, in a galaxy the size of a bus.

131
00:07:30,934 --> 00:07:31,780
So, our walk.

132
00:07:31,780 --> 00:07:33,731
The Earth, eleven centimeters.

133
00:07:33,731 --> 00:07:35,358
Voyager, eighteen meters.

134
00:07:35,358 --> 00:07:36,854
The Oort cloud, a town.

135
00:07:36,854 --> 00:07:39,065
The nearest star, a six hour hike.

136
00:07:39,065 --> 00:07:41,863
The Milky Way, wider than the Moon's orbit.

137
00:07:41,863 --> 00:07:48,107
And the edge, seven million years away on foot, but small enough to fit in our own solar system.

138
00:07:48,807 --> 00:07:56,886
Next time, we'll count how many Earths fit inside the biggest things in the universe, from the Sun to the largest black hole ever found.

139
00:07:56,886 --> 00:07:59,677
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

140
00:07:59,677 --> 00:08:03,954
And tell me in the comments, which stop on this walk surprised you most?
